title The field that became a surface
prompt Keaton: kg -- taking the SLC conversion of the essay's interactive field to Brushstroke for Skate.
rota lap 4314, row 4 -- Earth, the row that breathes in: read the concrete thing at the door before arguing about it. Here that meant reading what a .brush actually IS before converting anything into one.
think A .brush is not a program. It is a declarative surface layer -- `::` header pins, style tokens as flat Bron, a bounded list of lines, and a refuse list that names what it will never become. Its own tongue line says it plainly: Glow, Bron, TAME, Radiant; never JS, HTML, CSS, JSON.
think So the conversion is not a translation of the html's script. The html ran arithmetic in a browser; here the SURFACE holds the reading and the PHYSICS belongs to a Rye module, which is the design the previous lap wrote from the essay's own request.
obs THE ESSAY CARRIED ITS OWN WORKED CASE, INCLUDING A REFUSAL, WHICH IS WHAT MADE THIS COMPLETE RATHER THAN A SKETCH. Junction 100 C, coolant 10 K below, emissivity 0.90, sink 250 K, one face, self-view tax 15 percent -- and out of that, panel at 90 C, 585 W per m2 after the tax, 1.71 m2 per kW, 256 m2 for 150 kW, and AI1 peak at 1364 W per m2 over 110 m2 REFUSING, short by 779. A surface that draws a refusal is worth more than one that draws a number.
obs THE BOUND SHAPED THE DESIGN RATHER THAN THE DESIGN FIGHTING THE BOUND. brush_parse holds max_frame_lines at 8, so the reading had to say everything in eight lines. It does, exactly: three lines of inputs and five of readings, every one carrying a number and its unit. A ceiling met exactly is a ceiling that did its work.
obs I DECLINED TO MAKE THE INPUTS MOVE, AND THE HEADER SAYS WHY. A surface presenting a computed reading it cannot yet compute is a drawing of a promise. The inputs move when the Rye witness lands; until then this is complete AT ITS OWN SCOPE, which is the only honest kind of complete.
obs THE HTML ITSELF ADMITS THE SAME LIMIT, WHICH I ONLY FOUND BY READING IT. Its own line: "This viewer runs no scripts, so the field stands still at its first frame and the readings show the default case." So the artifact I converted was already showing a static worked case to most readers -- and the Brushstroke surface is not a downgrade of it but the same honesty in a tongue that never needed the script.
loom brush lines=8 bound=8 all_non_empty=yes non_ascii=10_tongue_separators parses=green
loom radiator_case junction_c=100 coolant_dk=10 emissivity=0.90 sink_k=250 tax_pct=15
loom radiator_case panel_c=90 shed_after_tax=585 wpm2 per_kw=1.71 m2 for_150kw=256 m2 refusal_short_by=779 wpm2
file brushstroke/radiator-reading.brush the essay's field as a Skate surface, with the refusal drawn
scope one Earth lap -- an interactive browser field converted to a bounded declarative surface, complete at its own scope
status GREEN -- brush_parse witness green including Skate grid headless, ascii documents ok, tracked links ok
recommend keep-going -- the Rye witness from 20260908.124500 is what makes the inputs move; it is diffuser's lane and a lap of days, and the sixteen Siya papers still want their own reading.
